1
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:05,959
Ask a general-purpose AI assistant about
Hume,

2
00:00:05,959 --> 00:00:09,130
and you'll usually get a fluent, helpful
answer.

3
00:00:09,835 --> 00:00:11,755
HumeBot takes a different approach.

4
00:00:12,402 --> 00:00:17,303
Instead of simply relying on the model to
behave well, it places the model

5
00:00:17,303 --> 00:00:22,402
inside a scholarly reading environment,
with safeguards enforced by software.

6
00:00:23,057 --> 00:00:24,327
Four commitments shape it.

7
00:00:25,211 --> 00:00:27,621
Explanations answer to Hume's own words.

8
00:00:28,322 --> 00:00:32,012
The process can be inspected, and
instructors keep authority.

9
00:00:32,632 --> 00:00:36,552
Students examine the evidence, and
challenge interpretations.

10
00:00:37,241 --> 00:00:41,581
And their questions and contributions
become lasting parts of the course.

11
00:00:42,792 --> 00:00:44,929
Let's follow one student's question:

12
00:00:44,929 --> 00:00:48,252
do we have reason to believe the sun will
rise tomorrow?

13
00:00:49,528 --> 00:00:52,878
First, HumeBot works out what a good
answer must address.

14
00:00:53,487 --> 00:00:56,267
Here, the model identified three
requirements.

15
00:00:57,336 --> 00:00:58,756
The grounds for the prediction.

16
00:00:59,461 --> 00:01:01,381
The origin of the expectation.

17
00:01:02,062 --> 00:01:04,002
And the status of our confidence in it.

18
00:01:06,151 --> 00:01:11,481
Next, it reads the whole controlled text:
two hundred and sixteen textual units.

19
00:01:12,072 --> 00:01:15,322
For the three requirements, it selects
twenty-four

20
00:01:15,322 --> 00:01:18,572
short passages, drawn from fifteen of
those units.

21
00:01:21,368 --> 00:01:23,708
The model then writes its explanation.

22
00:01:24,451 --> 00:01:29,781
Here are three of its steps, each tied by
citations to specific passages from Hume.

23
00:01:32,899 --> 00:01:35,719
AI systems can hallucinate quotations.

24
00:01:36,372 --> 00:01:40,342
Before showing them to students, HumeBot
independently checks

25
00:01:40,342 --> 00:01:44,442
the quotations and their citations against
its controlled text.

26
00:01:45,728 --> 00:01:49,208
Here, the cited words are found exactly
where the answer says.

27
00:01:52,857 --> 00:01:55,905
A quotation that cannot be found is
withheld,

28
00:01:55,905 --> 00:02:00,037
so invented evidence does not reach
students as Hume's words.

29
00:02:02,237 --> 00:02:05,808
And these words can be traced to their
source:

30
00:02:05,808 --> 00:02:10,077
a scholarly transcription of the 1777
edition, page 28,

31
00:02:11,552 --> 00:02:17,032
documented by research records made from
inspected scans of the original pages.

32
00:02:19,393 --> 00:02:21,979
Instructors can review every answer in the

33
00:02:21,979 --> 00:02:24,873
Evidence Inspector, which students cannot
open.

34
00:02:26,176 --> 00:02:29,206
What did HumeBot decide the answer needed
to address?

35
00:02:30,607 --> 00:02:32,987
The same three requirements we saw at the
start.

36
00:02:35,102 --> 00:02:37,102
What evidence did it select for each one?

37
00:02:38,827 --> 00:02:41,879
For the origin of the expectation, for
example,

38
00:02:41,879 --> 00:02:44,737
it selected this passage, from Section
five.

39
00:02:48,045 --> 00:02:52,465
Instructors can also see what was verified
before the answer was shown.

40
00:02:53,924 --> 00:02:57,464
The cited words matched Hume's text, and
nothing was withheld.

41
00:03:00,381 --> 00:03:03,191
Instructors can then evaluate the answer
itself.

42
00:03:04,556 --> 00:03:07,721
The review panel is designed to let
instructors

43
00:03:07,721 --> 00:03:10,953
judge different aspects of an answer
separately,

44
00:03:10,953 --> 00:03:14,522
such as philosophical accuracy, the
textual evidence,

45
00:03:14,522 --> 00:03:16,946
completeness, reasoning and clarity.

46
00:03:19,453 --> 00:03:24,079
An instructor can also mark whether a
passage was relevant to its requirement,

47
00:03:24,079 --> 00:03:27,103
and whether it supports the sentence that
cites it.

48
00:03:29,148 --> 00:03:33,262
When completed, instructor reviews can be
saved with the answer,

49
00:03:33,262 --> 00:03:36,348
creating a lasting record of scholarly
judgment.

50
00:03:39,442 --> 00:03:41,682
Students, too, can question the answer.

51
00:03:42,747 --> 00:03:46,377
The Reading Desk begins with the question
and the complete answer.

52
00:03:47,917 --> 00:03:50,387
A student picks out a claim: sentence
four.

53
00:03:52,412 --> 00:03:56,992
Then the student asks: does Hume mean that
our expectation is unreasonable?

54
00:03:58,242 --> 00:04:02,362
The question is saved with the course,
attached to that exact sentence.

55
00:04:03,737 --> 00:04:08,727
Classmates' questions and interpretations
about the same claim appear beside it.

56
00:04:10,871 --> 00:04:13,828
Selecting a citation opens Textual
Support:

57
00:04:13,828 --> 00:04:16,991
the passage, with the cited words
highlighted.

58
00:04:19,297 --> 00:04:23,107
Here, a student can ask whether this
passage really supports the claim.

59
00:04:25,344 --> 00:04:29,094
From there, the student can open Hume's
own text at that passage.

60
00:04:30,837 --> 00:04:33,932
The student can then read the surrounding
argument,

61
00:04:33,932 --> 00:04:36,117
beyond the excerpts the AI selected.

62
00:04:40,268 --> 00:04:42,458
Many answers cite the same passages.

63
00:04:43,709 --> 00:04:46,838
Three other recorded answers also cite
this one,

64
00:04:46,838 --> 00:04:50,879
from questions about custom, inference and
circular reasoning.

65
00:04:52,920 --> 00:04:55,980
In this environment, a student reading
this passage

66
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:59,280
can see how the same text was read for
other questions.

67
00:05:03,245 --> 00:05:06,047
The course also includes materials written

68
00:05:06,047 --> 00:05:08,915
by the instructor, independently of the
AI.

69
00:05:09,993 --> 00:05:13,843
They open in the same second pane, while
the answer stays in view.

70
00:05:15,326 --> 00:05:18,908
Here, the instructor's reconstruction of
Hume's argument,

71
00:05:18,908 --> 00:05:21,736
from the uniformity assumption to the
circle.

72
00:05:26,808 --> 00:05:32,788
Unlike a disposable chat, course activity
persists, in a PostgreSQL database.

73
00:05:33,943 --> 00:05:37,511
Questions, interpretations and scholarly
evaluations

74
00:05:37,511 --> 00:05:41,423
remain available for later inquiry and
instructor review.

75
00:05:45,362 --> 00:05:48,952
In sum, HumeBot combines AI-generated
explanations

76
00:05:48,952 --> 00:05:52,112
with independently checked textual
evidence,

77
00:05:52,112 --> 00:05:55,199
instructor oversight, and opportunities
for

78
00:05:55,199 --> 00:05:58,502
students to develop their own
interpretations.

79
00:06:00,261 --> 00:06:04,005
Its purpose is not simply to provide
answers about Hume,

80
00:06:04,005 --> 00:06:07,281
but to support independent philosophical
inquiry.

81
00:06:10,540 --> 00:06:17,063
HumeBot is a project by Mark Collier at
the University of Minnesota Morris,

82
00:06:17,063 --> 00:06:22,890
developed for PHIL 3171, History of Modern
Philosophy, Spring 2027.
